{
 "_meta": {
  "description": "Curated catalog of COMMONLY STOCKED off-the-shelf standard components: which product standards exist, in which sizes, and — for screws and pins — the discrete LENGTH LADDER that is actually stocked. This is a design-time corpus: it answers 'can I buy this part?', which no dimensional table can.",
  "market": "metric ISO/DIN general industrial distribution. Availability is a claim about a MARKET, not about physics: these ladders reflect what a general metric fastener distributor typically stocks. A regional or specialist supplier will carry more, and an inch-market supplier will carry different things entirely — this corpus does not model either.",
  "captured": "2026-07-15",
  "fidelity": "curated snapshot",
  "band_pct": 0.0,
  "source": "Nominal length values are the ISO 888 length series (screws) and the ISO 2338 length series (pins); per-diameter ladders are the commonly stocked span of that series, bounded below by the shortest length the head/thread geometry supports and above by where general distribution thins out. Size ranges are the size ranges of each product standard, trimmed to what is generally stocked. Property classes/materials are ISO 898-1 (screws), ISO 898-2 (nuts), ISO 898-5 (set screws), ISO 3506 (stainless) and ISO 7089/7093 hardness classes.",
  "not_covered": [
   "ISO 3601 METRIC O-ring sizes — the metric size table is not an arithmetic run that can be reproduced from anchors the way AS568 is, and shipping it half-right would be worse than shipping AS568 alone. Only AS568 inch dash sizes are covered.",
   "Inch/UNC/UNF fasteners entirely.",
   "Screws above M20 and washers above nominal 36.",
   "Non-preferred intermediate lengths (14, 18, 22, 28 mm on screws): they are in the ISO 888 series and are made, but are not generally stocked, so they are deliberately absent from the ladders.",
   "Coatings and platings (zinc, black oxide, PTFE) — a finish, not a size."
  ]
